# The pinned BLIS stack: the ONE source of truth for "which simulator priced these reports".
#
# Read by scripts/build_catalog.py and every workflow in .github/workflows/; SETUP.md documents
# the same pins for a human cloning by hand.
#
# Shell-sourceable (`set -a; . pins.env; set +a`) and parsed as plain KEY=VALUE by Python, so
# keep to unquoted, comment-or-assignment lines.
#
# Bumping a pin is automated: bump-blis-pin.yml opens a DRAFT PR (`scripts/blis_pin.py bump`),
# pin-compat.yml gates it, and merging runs regen-reports.yml. By hand: change BLIS_REF/BLIS_SHA,
# run `python3 -I scripts/sync_pins.py` for the derived block, then `scripts/test_build.py`.

# The simulator whose binary prices every run. BLIS_SHA is a COMMIT, and deliberately not a binary
# digest: the same ref builds to different bytes per toolchain, so a pinned digest would be wrong
# for every contributor but its author. The runners resolve the binary's SOURCE commit and compare
# that. CI refuses a ref that does not resolve to BLIS_SHA, while a local sweep only warns -- testing
# against an unreleased simulator is how a pin bump gets evaluated.
#
# M3.2: BLIS_REF is the M3.2 stack head, inference-sim's m32/final-pass-matrix (pull request #2671),
# named by its commit, which is also BLIS_SHA. It moves to the release tag once M3.2 is tagged: set
# BLIS_REF to the tag and BLIS_SHA to the commit the tag peels to. Until then the build is NOT a tagged
# release (scripts/build_blis.sh stamps only a plain release tag), so every Result says
# data.build "unreleased" and released_data false, and run_experiment calls a report priced by it
# experimental and refuses to write it to reports/. That is intended: the committed reports are
# regenerated by regen-reports.yml after the tag, never by a head build.
BLIS_REF=3e8fb1d24106c001f4eb46ce96cb219b7125675e
BLIS_SHA=3e8fb1d24106c001f4eb46ce96cb219b7125675e

# The router's reference EndpointPicker configs and their index are embedded in the pinned simulator and
# read with `blis router-configs --json`; they have no pin of their own (BLIS_SHA determines them).

# The catalog and registry a committed report is priced with are the ones EMBEDDED in the pinned
# simulator (CFG-1): the binary is run with no --catalog or --registry, and every Result records data
# source "embedded". They have no pin of their own. The scripts that read catalog and registry FILES
# (workload descriptors, model graphs) read them from the same commit's substrate/ directory
# (scripts/clone_stack.sh), which is the same data.

# Since M3.2 blis-substrate, blis-vllm and blis-sglang are directories of inference-sim itself
# (substrate/, engines/vllm, engines/sglang), so BLIS_SHA determines them and none has a pin of its
# own. Only the router fork is still a separate module: sync_pins.py derives it from inference-sim's
# go.mod at BLIS_SHA into the block below.

# >>> derived from inference-sim's go.mod at BLIS_SHA -- DO NOT EDIT BY HAND >>>
# Regenerate with: python3 -I scripts/sync_pins.py  (after changing BLIS_SHA above)
# Guarded by test_build.py::test_derived_pins_match_gomod.
ROUTER_REF=v1.0.0-blis.5.0.20261121014725-cb8d93fcb0fa
# <<< end derived block <<<
